verygoddamnday.com/2015/07/chicago-fire-week-2-floating-firehouse.html"}, {"title": "Maritime History of the Great Lakes", "url": "https://images.maritimehistoryofthegreatlakes.ca/21856/data?g=d"}]}, "The Chicago fireboat in question is the **Illinois**, and the search results support the 1908 sinking and quick recovery.\n\n- **The sinking:** A 1908 newspaper account says the boat went down at 8:30 a.m. on a Tuesday, the day after a major fire at the Chicago, Burlington & Quincy railroad dock at 10th Street. Its sinking came after a grain elevator wall collapsed onto it, and several firemen were slightly hurt. (Sources: Valentine Democrat, Aug. 13, 1908, and an Aug. 6, 1908 Nebraska newspaper.)\n- **The recovery:** Wikipedia says the Illinois sank in about 20 feet of water in the Chicago River and was quickly refloated and returned to service. (Source: Wikipedia, \"Illinois (fireboat),\" and the \"Fireboats of Chicago\" article's fleet table.)\n- **Discrepancy:** The commissioning date conflicts across sources. The Illinois article says 1888, while the fleet table in \"Fireboats of Chicago\" lists 1899 and describes it as the city's first steel-hulled fireboat. I didn't find a resolution.\n\nI only ran one search, so I haven't verified the refloating details against a primary source beyond Wikipedia."], "durationSeconds": 5.215495999999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01SmnHrHNJkGt3RhYpQNoo6b", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
